window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-3e83237203418633"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"best-practices\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"_1\">最佳实践</h1> <blockquote> <p>如需完整文档索引，请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在文档页面 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p>如果你刚开始使用 Codex 或其他编程智能体，本指南可以帮助你更快获得更好的结果。它介绍如何在 <a href=\"#/article/a-24d888be8e5fa72d\">CLI</a>、<a href=\"#/article/a-c7af5bbd57109077\">IDE 扩展</a>和 <a href=\"#/article/a-d6f4398aa4652d7e\">ChatGPT 桌面应用</a>中更有效地使用 Codex，内容涵盖提示词、计划、验证、MCP、技能和计划任务等核心习惯。</p> <p>与其把 Codex 当作一次性助手，不如把它看成一位可以持续配置和改进的队友，这样效果会更好。</p> <p>可以这样理解：先为任务准备合适的上下文，用 <code>AGENTS.md</code> 保存长期适用的指导，根据自己的工作方式配置 Codex，通过 MCP 连接外部系统，把重复工作整理成技能，并将稳定的工作流自动化。</p> <p><a id=\"strong-first-use-context-and-prompts\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">第一次使用时的关键：上下文和提示词</h2> <p>即使你的提示词不够完美，Codex 也已经能很好地帮上忙。你通常只需少量设置，就可以把困难问题交给它并获得不错的结果。清晰的<a href=\"#/article/a-0310706ee8617b64\">提示词</a>不是获得价值的必要条件，但在大型代码库或风险较高的任务中，确实能让结果更可靠。</p> <p>如果你在大型或复杂的代码库中工作，最重要的提升方式就是为 Codex 提供正确的上下文，并明确说明你希望它完成什么。</p> <p>一个实用的默认提示词可以包含以下四项：</p> <ul> <li><strong>目标：</strong> 你想要修改或构建什么？</li> <li><strong>上下文：</strong> 哪些文件、文件夹、文档、示例或错误信息与任务有关？你可以 @ 提及文件作为上下文。</li> <li><strong>约束：</strong> Codex 应遵循哪些标准、架构、安全要求或约定？</li> <li><strong>完成标准：</strong> 完成任务时应该满足什么条件？例如测试通过、行为发生变化，或某个错误不再出现。</li> </ul> <p>这些信息可以帮助 Codex 控制任务范围、减少假设，并让结果更容易审查。</p> <p>如果你的账户和客户端可以使用 GPT-6.1 Sol，可从客户端默认提供的推理力度开始。GPT-6 Luna 建议先从 <strong>High</strong> 开始；GPT-6 Astra 建议先用 <strong>Light</strong>（配置中的 <code>low</code>）。有关可用推理级别，请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-ae24d057324a4938\">Models</a>。</p> <ul> <li>对范围明确、简单的任务，使用 Low，速度更快。</li> <li>对更复杂的修改或调试，使用 Medium 或 High。</li> <li>对耗时较长、需要智能体进行大量推理的任务，使用 Extra High。</li> </ul> <p>要更快地提供上下文，可以在 ChatGPT 桌面应用中使用语音听写来描述任务，而不必逐字输入。</p> <p><a id=\"plan-first-for-difficult-tasks\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">困难任务先制定计划</h2> <p>如果任务复杂、含糊或难以描述，可以先让 Codex 制定计划，再开始编写代码。</p> <p>有几种方法：</p> <p><strong>使用 Plan 模式：</strong> 对大多数用户来说，这是最简单、最有效的方法。Plan 模式可以让 Codex 收集上下文、提出澄清问题，并制定更可靠的计划。使用 <code>/plan</code> 或 <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd> 切换。</p> <p><strong>请 Codex 先问清需求：</strong> 如果你的想法还比较粗略、自己也不确定如何描述，可以让 Codex 先采访你。告诉它要挑战你的假设，并在写入之前将模糊想法整理为明确要求。</p> <p><strong>使用 PLANS.md 模板：</strong> 对更复杂的工作，可以设置 Codex 遵循 <code>PLANS.md</code> 或执行计划模板。更多详情请参阅<a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/cookbook/articles/codex_exec_plans.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">执行计划指南</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"make-guidance-reusable-with-agentsmd\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"agentsmd\">用 <code>AGENTS.md</code> 让指导可重复使用</h2> <p>一种提示词模式经验证有效后，下一步就是不要再手动重复输入。可以使用 <a href=\"#/article/a-94f17e3de1ca7cb3\"><code>AGENTS.md</code></a>。你可以把 <code>AGENTS.md</code> 看成面向智能体的开放格式 README；它会自动加载，也是记录你和团队希望如何协作的最佳位置。</p> <p>一份好的 <code>AGENTS.md</code> 可以说明：</p> <ul> <li>仓库布局和重要目录</li> <li>如何运行项目</li> <li>构建、测试和 lint 命令</li> <li>工程约定和拉取请求要求</li> <li>限制和禁止事项</li> <li>如何判断工作已经完成</li> </ul> <p>CLI 中的 <code>/init</code> 命令可以快速创建一份基础 <code>AGENTS.md</code>，但最好继续编辑它，让内容符合项目真实的构建、测试、审查方式和约定。</p> <p>可以在不同层级创建 <code>AGENTS.md</code> 文件：用于个人默认设置的全局 <code>AGENTS.md</code> 应放在 <code>~/.codex</code> 中；此外还可以在项目中创建仓库级文件，以及更具体的本地文件。如果某个目录附近有更具体的文件，以更具体的指导为准。</p> <p>保持内容实用。简短而准确的指导，比充满模糊套话的长文件更有用。如果 <code>AGENTS.md</code> 开始变得太长，可以保持主文件简洁，并链接到任务专用说明，例如计划、代码审查或架构文档。</p> <p>如果 Codex 重复犯同一个错误，可以请它复盘，并据此更新 <code>AGENTS.md</code>。指导应建立在真实摩擦点上，并持续保持实用。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-codex-for-consistency\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"codex\">配置 Codex，保持行为一致</h2> <p>配置是让 Codex 行为更一致的主要方法之一。例如，可以设置默认模型、推理力度、沙盒模式、审批策略、配置档案和 MCP。</p> <p>一个好的起点是：</p> <ul> <li>将个人默认设置放在 <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code> 中（在 ChatGPT 桌面应用中选择 <strong>Settings &gt; Configuration &gt; Open config.toml</strong>）。</li> <li>将仓库专用设置放在 <code>.codex/config.toml</code> 中。</li> <li>仅在当前任务需要时使用命令行覆盖项（如果你使用 CLI）。</li> </ul> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76\"><code>config.toml</code></a> 是设置 MCP 服务器、多智能体配置和功能标志等长期偏好的位置。不同配置档案的覆盖项放在单独的 <code>$CODEX_HOME/profile-name.config.toml</code> 文件中。</p> <p>Codex 自带操作系统级沙盒机制，你可以通过两个主要设置控制它。审批模式决定 Codex 在何时请求批准；沙盒模式决定它能读取或写入哪些目录和文件。</p> <p>如果你刚开始使用编程智能体，建议先使用默认权限。默认情况下收紧审批和沙盒设置；只有在可信仓库或特定工作确实需要时，再适当放宽。</p> <p>注意：CLI、IDE 扩展和 ChatGPT 桌面应用共享相同的配置层。更多信息请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-f52101a0e13391a3\">示例配置</a>。</p> <p>尽早根据真实环境配置 Codex。很多质量问题其实是设置问题，例如工作目录不正确、缺少写入权限、默认模型不合适，或缺少所需工具和连接器。</p> <p><a id=\"improve-reliability-with-testing-and-review\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">通过测试和审查提升可靠性</h2> <p>不要只让 Codex 完成修改就停止。必要时请它创建测试、运行相关检查、确认结果，并在接受修改前审查代码。</p> <p>只有当 Codex 知道什么才算“正确”时，它才能可靠地完成这套流程。这些要求可以放在提示词或 <code>AGENTS.md</code> 中。</p> <p>你可以要求它：</p> <ul> <li>为修改编写或更新测试。</li> <li>运行合适的测试套件。</li> <li>检查 lint、格式或类型检查。</li> <li>确认最终行为符合请求。</li> <li>审查工作成果，找出错误、回归问题或风险较高的改动。</li> </ul> <p>在 ChatGPT 桌面应用中打开差异面板，可以直接在本地<a href=\"#/article/a-47b0440a51c2fc92\">审查修改</a>。单击某一行即可提供反馈，这些反馈会作为上下文传给 Codex 的下一轮。</p> <p>一个实用的选项是斜杠命令 <code>/review</code>，它提供多种审查方式：</p> <ul> <li>对照基准分支进行类似拉取请求的审查。</li> <li>审查未提交的修改。</li> <li>审查某个提交。</li> <li>使用自定义审查指令。</li> </ul> <p>如果团队有 <code>code_review.md</code> 文件，并在 <code>AGENTS.md</code> 中引用它，Codex 也可以遵循其中的指导。这对于希望保持审查行为一致的团队特别有用。</p> <p>Codex 不只是生成代码。只要给它合适的指令，它也能帮助你<strong>测试、检查和审查代码</strong>。</p> <p>如果你使用 GitHub Cloud，可以设置 Codex 为拉取请求运行<a href=\"#/article/a-ba7354e174823641\">代码审查</a>。在 OpenAI，Codex 会审查 100% 的拉取请求。你可以启用自动审查，也可以在拉取请求中 @Codex，按需发起审查。</p> <p><a id=\"use-mcps-for-external-context\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"mcp\">使用 MCP 获取外部上下文</h2> <p>如果 Codex 需要的上下文在代码仓库之外，可以使用 MCP。这样它就能连接你已有的工具和系统，不必再把实时信息复制粘贴到提示词中。</p> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">Model Context Protocol</a>（模型上下文协议，MCP）是一种开放标准，用于将 Codex 连接到外部工具和系统。</p> <p>适合使用 MCP 的情况：</p> <ul> <li>所需上下文位于代码仓库之外。</li> <li>数据会频繁变化。</li> <li>你希望 Codex 使用工具，而不是依赖粘贴的指令。</li> <li>你需要可以在多个用户之间重复使用的集成。</li> </ul> <p>Codex 支持 STDIO 和 Streamable HTTP 服务器，并支持 OAuth。</p> <p>在 ChatGPT 桌面应用中，打开 <strong>Settings &gt; MCP servers</strong>，查看自定义和推荐的服务器。很多时候，你可以直接请 Codex 帮助安装所需服务器。你也可以在 CLI 中使用 <code>codex mcp add</code> 命令，添加带有名称和 URL 的自定义服务器。</p> <p>只添加能解锁实际工作流的工具。不要一开始就连接你用到的所有工具；先从一两个能明显减少手动步骤的工具开始，再逐步扩展。</p> <p><a id=\"turn-repeatable-work-into-skills\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">将重复工作整理成技能</h2> <p>当某项工作流变得可重复时，不要再依赖很长的提示词或反复沟通。可以使用<a href=\"#/article/a-575ba4762ea69b23\">技能</a>，把指令、上下文以及 Codex 应一致遵循的辅助逻辑打包到 <code>SKILL.md</code> 文件中。技能可在 CLI、IDE 扩展和 ChatGPT 桌面应用中使用。</p> <p>每个技能只负责一类工作。先定义 2 到 3 个具体用例，明确输入和输出，再撰写说明，写清它能做什么以及何时使用。描述中应包含用户实际会说的触发短语。</p> <p>不要一开始就覆盖所有边缘情况。先让一个有代表性的任务运行良好，再把流程整理成技能并持续改进。只有脚本或额外资源确实能提高可靠性时，才将它们加入技能。</p> <p>一个实用的判断方法是：如果你反复使用同一提示词，或反复纠正同一种工作流，它很可能应该变成一个技能。</p> <p>适合用技能封装的重复工作包括：</p> <ul> <li>日志分流和初步诊断。</li> <li>起草发布说明。</li> <li>根据检查清单审查拉取请求。</li> <li>制定迁移计划。</li> <li>汇总遥测数据或事件。</li> <li>标准化调试流程。</li> </ul> <p>如果要创建技能，可以从 <code>$skill-creator</code> 开始，使用它生成初始结构。迭代期间先把技能保留在本地；准备好分享时，再将它打包成<a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/plugins.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">插件</a>。技能最重要的部分之一是说明，它应明确技能的作用和适用时机。</p> <p>个人技能保存在 <code>$HOME/.agents/skills</code> 中；共享技能可以保存在仓库里的 <code>.agents/skills</code>。这对新成员熟悉项目尤其有帮助。</p> <p><a id=\"use-scheduled-tasks-for-repeated-work\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_6\">使用计划任务处理重复工作</h2> <p>工作流稳定后，可以让它定期在后台运行。在 ChatGPT 桌面应用的<a href=\"#/article/a-b5406e60cbfa0b68\">计划任务</a>页面中，可以选择项目、提示词、频率，以及在专用 Git 工作树还是本地环境中运行。计划任务可以调用技能。更多详情请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-d955137d94c4d306\">Git 工作树</a>。</p> <p>适合安排计划任务的工作包括：</p> <ul> <li>总结最近的提交。</li> <li>扫描可能的错误。</li> <li>起草发布说明。</li> <li>检查 CI 失败。</li> <li>生成每日站会摘要。</li> <li>运行可重复的分析工作流。</li> </ul> <p>技能定义方法，计划任务定义运行时间。若工作流仍需要大量指导，先把方法整理成技能。等流程可以稳定运行后，再安排计划任务。</p> <p>计划任务不仅适合执行，也可用于反思和维护。你可以定期回顾近期任务，总结反复出现的问题，并改进提示词、指令或设置。</p> <p><a id=\"organize-long-running-tasks\"></a></p> <p><a id=\"organize-long-running-chats\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_7\">整理长期任务</h2> <p>聊天会不断积累上下文、决定和操作记录，因此整理任务对长期工作有很大帮助。ChatGPT 桌面应用允许你置顶聊天并创建工作树。CLI 用户可以使用以下<a href=\"#/article/a-820e54e1b31b71d3\">斜杠命令</a>：</p> <ul> <li><code>/experimental</code>：切换实验功能并添加到 <code>config.toml</code>。</li> <li><code>/resume</code>：恢复已保存的聊天。</li> <li><code>/fork</code>：创建分支聊天，同时保留原对话。</li> <li><code>/compact</code>：压缩较长的聊天，以便你继续使用先前的上下文。Codex 也会自动压缩聊天。</li> <li><code>/subagents</code>：切换到当前聊天中的子智能体线程。</li> <li><code>/theme</code>：选择语法高亮主题。</li> <li><code>/apps</code>：在 Codex 中直接使用 ChatGPT 应用。</li> <li><code>/status</code>：查看当前会话状态。</li> </ul> <p>一项连贯的工作尽量使用一个聊天。如果仍在解决同一问题，继续使用同一聊天通常更好，因为它可以保留推理过程。只有当工作确实分成不同方向时才创建分支。</p> <p>可以使用 Codex 的<a href=\"#/article/a-731168d993ec5d2b\">子智能体</a>工作流，委派范围明确的子任务。让主智能体专注于核心工作；探索、测试或问题分类等任务可以交给子智能体。</p> <p><a id=\"common-mistakes\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_8\">常见错误</h2> <p>以下情况应尽量避免：</p> <ul> <li>把长期有效的规则都塞进提示词，而不是写进 <code>AGENTS.md</code> 或技能。</li> <li>没有提供项目的构建和测试命令等运行说明，导致智能体无法验证自己的工作效果。</li> <li>复杂任务没有先制定计划。</li> <li>还不了解工作流时，就给智能体开放整台计算机的权限。</li> <li>没有使用 Git 工作树，却让多个实时任务同时修改同一批文件。</li> <li>计划任务尚不稳定，就过早安排定期运行。</li> <li>一个聊天涵盖整个项目，而不是一个连贯的结果；这会让上下文膨胀、降低质量。</li> </ul>","en":"<h1 id=\"best-practices\">Best practices</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <p>If you’re new to Codex or coding agents in general, this guide will help you get better results faster. It covers the core habits that make Codex more effective across the <a href=\"#/article/a-24d888be8e5fa72d\">CLI</a>, <a href=\"#/article/a-c7af5bbd57109077\">IDE extension</a>, and the <a href=\"#/article/a-d6f4398aa4652d7e\">ChatGPT desktop app</a>, from prompting and planning to validation, MCP, skills, and scheduled tasks.</p> <p>Codex works best when you treat it less like a one-off assistant and more like a teammate you configure and improve over time.</p> <p>A useful way to think about this: start with the right context for the task, use <code>AGENTS.md</code> for durable guidance, configure Codex to match your workflow, connect external systems with MCP, turn repeated work into skills, and automate stable workflows.</p> <h2 id=\"strong-first-use-context-and-prompts\">Strong first use: Context and prompts</h2> <p>Codex is already strong enough to be useful even when your prompt isn't perfect. You can often hand it a hard problem with minimal setup and still get a strong result. Clear <a href=\"#/article/a-0310706ee8617b64\">prompting</a> isn't required to get value, but it does make results more reliable, especially in larger codebases or higher-stakes tasks.</p> <p>If you work in a large or complex repository, the biggest unlock is giving Codex the right context for the task and a clear structure for what you want done.</p> <p>A good default is to include four things in your prompt:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Goal:</strong> What are you trying to change or build?</li> <li><strong>Context:</strong> Which files, folders, docs, examples, or errors matter for this task? You can @ mention certain files as context.</li> <li><strong>Constraints:</strong> What standards, architecture, safety requirements, or conventions should Codex follow?</li> <li><strong>Done when:</strong> What should be true before the task is complete, such as tests passing, behavior changing, or a bug no longer reproducing?</li> </ul> <p>This helps Codex stay scoped, make fewer assumptions, and produce work that's easier to review.</p> <p>Use GPT-6.1 Sol when available to your account and client, starting with the reasoning effort available by default in your client. Start with <strong>High</strong> for GPT-6 Luna or <strong>Light</strong> for GPT-6 Astra (<code>low</code> in configuration). Adjust based on the task and the result. See <a href=\"#/article/a-ae24d057324a4938\">Models</a> for available reasoning levels.</p> <ul> <li>Low for faster, well-scoped tasks</li> <li>Medium or High for more complex changes or debugging</li> <li>Extra High for long, agentic, reasoning-heavy tasks</li> </ul> <p>To provide context faster, try using speech dictation inside the ChatGPT desktop app to dictate what you want Codex to do rather than typing it.</p> <h2 id=\"plan-first-for-difficult-tasks\">Plan first for difficult tasks</h2> <p>If the task is complex, ambiguous, or hard to describe well, ask Codex to plan before it starts coding.</p> <p>A few approaches work well:</p> <p><strong>Use Plan mode:</strong> For most users, this is the easiest and most effective option. Plan mode lets Codex gather context, ask clarifying questions, and build a stronger plan before implementation. Toggle with <code>/plan</code> or <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd>.</p> <p><strong>Ask Codex to interview you:</strong> If you have a rough idea of what you want but aren't sure how to describe it well, ask Codex to question you first. Tell it to challenge your assumptions and turn the fuzzy idea into something concrete before writing code.</p> <p><strong>Use a PLANS.md template:</strong> For more advanced workflows, you can configure Codex to follow a <code>PLANS.md</code> or execution-plan template for longer-running or multi-step work. For more detail, see the <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/cookbook/articles/codex_exec_plans.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">execution plans guide</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"make-guidance-reusable-with-agentsmd\">Make guidance reusable with <code>AGENTS.md</code></h2> <p>Once a prompting pattern works, the next step is to stop repeating it manually. That's where <a href=\"#/article/a-94f17e3de1ca7cb3\">AGENTS.md</a> comes in.</p> <p>Think of <code>AGENTS.md</code> as an open-format README for agents. It loads into context automatically and is the best place to encode how you and your team want Codex to work in a repository.</p> <p>A good <code>AGENTS.md</code> covers:</p> <ul> <li>repo layout and important directories</li> <li>How to run the project</li> <li>Build, test, and lint commands</li> <li>Engineering conventions and PR expectations</li> <li>Constraints and do-not rules</li> <li>What done means and how to verify work</li> </ul> <p>The <code>/init</code> slash command in the CLI is the quick-start command to scaffold a starter <code>AGENTS.md</code> in the current directory. It's a great starting point, but you should edit the result to match how your team actually builds, tests, reviews, and ships code.</p> <p>You can create <code>AGENTS.md</code> files at different levels: a global <code>AGENTS.md</code> for personal defaults that sits in <code>~/.codex</code>, a repo-level file for shared standards, and more specific files in subdirectories for local rules. If there’s a more specific file closer to your current directory, that guidance wins.</p> <p>Keep it practical. A short, accurate <code>AGENTS.md</code> is more useful than a long file full of vague rules. Start with the basics, then add new rules only after you notice repeated mistakes.</p> <p>If <code>AGENTS.md</code> starts getting too large, keep the main file concise and reference task-specific markdown files for things like planning, code review, or architecture.</p> <p>When Codex makes the same mistake twice, ask it for a retrospective and update <code>AGENTS.md</code>. Guidance stays practical and based on real friction.</p> <h2 id=\"configure-codex-for-consistency\">Configure Codex for consistency</h2> <p>Configuration is one of the main ways to make Codex behave more consistently across sessions and surfaces. For example, you can set defaults for model choice, reasoning effort, sandbox mode, approval policy, profiles, and MCP setup.</p> <p>A good starting pattern is:</p> <ul> <li>Keep personal defaults in <code>~/.codex/config.toml</code> (<strong>Settings &gt; Configuration &gt; Open config.toml</strong> in the ChatGPT desktop app)</li> <li>Keep repo-specific behavior in <code>.codex/config.toml</code></li> <li>Use command-line overrides only for one-off situations (if you use the CLI)</li> </ul> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76\"><code>config.toml</code></a> is where you define durable preferences such as MCP servers, multi-agent setup, and feature flags. Profile-specific overrides live in separate <code>$CODEX_HOME/profile-name.config.toml</code> files.</p> <p>Codex ships with operating level sandboxing and has two key knobs that you can control. Approval mode determines when Codex asks for your permission to run a command and sandbox mode determines if Codex can read or write in the directory and what files the agent can access.</p> <p>If you're new to coding agents, start with the default permissions. Keep approval and sandboxing tight by default, then loosen permissions only for trusted repos or specific workflows once the need is clear.</p> <p>Note that the CLI, IDE extension, and ChatGPT desktop app all share the same configuration layers. Learn more on the <a href=\"#/article/a-f52101a0e13391a3\">sample configuration</a> page.</p> <p>Configure Codex for your real environment early. Many quality issues are really setup issues, like the wrong working directory, missing write access, wrong model defaults, or missing tools and connectors.</p> <h2 id=\"improve-reliability-with-testing-and-review\">Improve reliability with testing and review</h2> <p>Don't stop at asking Codex to make a change. Ask it to create tests when needed, run the relevant checks, confirm the result, and review the work before you accept it.</p> <p>Codex can do this loop for you, but only if it knows what “good” looks like. That guidance can come from either the prompt or <code>AGENTS.md</code>.</p> <p>That can include:</p> <ul> <li>Writing or updating tests for the change</li> <li>Running the right test suites</li> <li>Checking lint, formatting, or type checks</li> <li>Confirming the final behavior matches the request</li> <li>Reviewing the diff for bugs, regressions, or risky patterns</li> </ul> <p>Toggle the diff panel in the ChatGPT desktop app to directly <a href=\"#/article/a-47b0440a51c2fc92\">review changes</a> locally. Click on a specific row to provide feedback that gets fed as context to the next Codex turn.</p> <p>A useful option here is the slash command <code>/review</code>, which gives you a few ways to review code:</p> <ul> <li>Review against a base branch for PR-style review</li> <li>Review uncommitted changes</li> <li>Review a commit</li> <li>Use custom review instructions</li> </ul> <p>If you and your team have a <code>code_review.md</code> file and reference it from <code>AGENTS.md</code>, Codex can follow that guidance during review as well. This is a strong pattern for teams that want review behavior to stay consistent across repositories and contributors.</p> <p>Codex shouldn't just generate code. With the right instructions, it can also help <strong>test it, check it, and review it</strong>.</p> <p>If you use GitHub Cloud, you can set up Codex to run <a href=\"#/article/a-ba7354e174823641\">code reviews for your PRs</a>. At OpenAI, Codex reviews 100% of PRs. You can enable automatic reviews or have Codex reactively review when you @Codex.</p> <h2 id=\"use-mcps-for-external-context\">Use MCPs for external context</h2> <p>Use MCPs when the context Codex needs lives outside the repo. It lets Codex connect to the tools and systems you already use, so you don't have to keep copying and pasting live information into prompts.</p> <p><a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">Model Context Protocol</a>, or MCP, is an open standard for connecting Codex to external tools and systems.</p> <p>Use MCP when:</p> <ul> <li>The needed context lives outside the repo</li> <li>The data changes frequently</li> <li>You want Codex to use a tool rather than rely on pasted instructions</li> <li>You need a repeatable integration across users or projects</li> </ul> <p>Codex supports both STDIO and Streamable HTTP servers with OAuth.</p> <p>In the ChatGPT desktop app, go to <strong>Settings &gt; MCP servers</strong> to see custom and recommended servers. Often, Codex can help you install the needed servers. All you need to do is ask. You can also use the <code>codex mcp add</code> command in the CLI to add your custom servers with a name, URL, and other details.</p> <p>Add tools only when they unlock a real workflow. Do not start by wiring in every tool you use. Start with one or two tools that clearly remove a manual loop you already do often, then expand from there.</p> <h2 id=\"turn-repeatable-work-into-skills\">Turn repeatable work into skills</h2> <p>Once a workflow becomes repeatable, stop relying on long prompts or repeated back-and-forth. Use a <a href=\"#/article/a-575ba4762ea69b23\">skill</a> to package the instructions in a <code>SKILL.md</code> file, context, and supporting logic Codex should apply consistently. Skills work across the CLI, IDE extension, and ChatGPT desktop app.</p> <p>Keep each skill scoped to one job. Start with 2 to 3 concrete use cases, define clear inputs and outputs, and write the description so it says what the skill does and when to use it. Include the kinds of trigger phrases a user would actually say.</p> <p>Don't try to cover every edge case up front. Start with one representative task, get it working well, then turn that workflow into a skill and improve from there. Include scripts or extra assets only when they improve reliability.</p> <p>A good rule of thumb: if you keep reusing the same prompt or correcting the same workflow, it should probably become a skill.</p> <p>Skills are especially useful for recurring jobs like:</p> <ul> <li>Log triage</li> <li>Release note drafting</li> <li>PR review against a checklist</li> <li>Migration planning</li> <li>Telemetry or incident summaries</li> <li>Standard debugging flows</li> </ul> <p>The <code>$skill-creator</code> skill is the best place to start to scaffold the first version of a skill. Keep the first version local while you iterate. When it's ready to share broadly, package it as a <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/plugins.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">plugin</a>. One of the most important parts of a skill is the description. It should say what the skill does and when to use it.</p> <p>Personal skills are stored in <code>$HOME/.agents/skills</code>, and shared team skills can be checked into <code>.agents/skills</code> inside a repository. This is especially helpful for onboarding new teammates.</p> <h2 id=\"use-scheduled-tasks-for-repeated-work\">Use scheduled tasks for repeated work</h2> <p>Once a workflow is stable, you can schedule Codex to run it in the background for you. In the ChatGPT desktop app, <a href=\"#/article/a-b5406e60cbfa0b68\">scheduled tasks</a> let you choose the project, prompt, cadence, and execution environment for recurring work.</p> <p>Create a scheduled task from the <strong>Scheduled</strong> page. Choose the project, prompt, cadence, and whether the task runs in a dedicated Git worktree or in your local environment. The prompt can invoke skills. Learn more about <a href=\"#/article/a-d955137d94c4d306\">Git worktrees</a>.</p> <p>Good candidates include:</p> <ul> <li>Summarizing recent commits</li> <li>Scanning for likely bugs</li> <li>Drafting release notes</li> <li>Checking CI failures</li> <li>Producing standup summaries</li> <li>Running repeatable analysis workflows on a schedule</li> </ul> <p>A useful rule is that skills define the method and scheduled tasks define the schedule. If a workflow still needs a lot of steering, turn it into a skill first. Once it's predictable, scheduling it can save time.</p> <p>Use scheduled tasks for reflection and maintenance, not just execution. Review recent chats, summarize repeated friction, and improve prompts, instructions, or workflow setup over time.</p> <p><a id=\"organize-long-running-tasks\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"organize-long-running-chats\">Organize long-running chats</h2> <p>Chats accumulate context, decisions, and actions over time, so managing them well has a big impact on quality.</p> <p>The ChatGPT desktop app lets you pin chats and create worktrees. If you use the CLI, these <a href=\"#/article/a-820e54e1b31b71d3\">slash commands</a> are especially useful:</p> <ul> <li><code>/experimental</code> to toggle experimental features and add to your <code>config.toml</code></li> <li><code>/resume</code> to resume a saved chat</li> <li><code>/fork</code> to create a new chat while preserving the original transcript</li> <li><code>/compact</code> when the chat is getting long and you want a summarized version of earlier context. Codex also compacts chats automatically</li> <li><code>/subagents</code> to switch between the current chat’s subagent threads</li> <li><code>/theme</code> to choose a syntax highlighting theme</li> <li><code>/apps</code> to use ChatGPT apps directly in Codex</li> <li><code>/status</code> to inspect the current session state</li> </ul> <p>Keep one chat per coherent unit of work. If the work is still part of the same problem, staying in the same chat is often better because it preserves the reasoning trail. Fork only when the work truly branches.</p> <p>Use Codex’s <a href=\"#/article/a-731168d993ec5d2b\">subagent</a> workflows to offload bounded work from the main thread. Keep the main agent focused on the core problem, and use subagents for tasks like exploration, tests, or triage.</p> <h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2> <p>A few common mistakes to avoid when first using Codex:</p> <ul> <li>Overloading the prompt with durable rules instead of moving them into <code>AGENTS.md</code> or a skill</li> <li>Not letting the agent see its work by not giving details on how to best run build and test commands</li> <li>Skipping planning on multi-step and complex tasks</li> <li>Giving Codex full permission to your computer before you understand the workflow</li> <li>Running live tasks on the same files without using Git worktrees</li> <li>Scheduling a recurring task before it's reliable manually</li> <li>Treating Codex like something you have to watch step by step instead of using it in parallel with your own work</li> <li>Using one chat for an entire project instead of one chat per coherent outcome. This leads to bloated context and worse results over time</li> </ul>"};
